{"paths": ["governance/MARK_DECISION_PARENT_MICROCLUSTER_RULE_2026-08-20.md"], "blob_sha": "006cbb47663ee8e0f79ca93943de0e63093d068e", "original_offset": 0, "original_length": 1786, "final_blob_size": 1786, "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_count": 1, "classification": "text", "content_utf8": "# MARK DECISION PARENT / MICROCLUSTER RULE\n\nDate: 2026-08-20\nStatus: ACTIVE\n\n## Core rule\nDuring Mark A/B/C review, one physical parent site / compound / ashram / hermitage / station / temple complex / hospital campus / historic property gets ONE Mark decision number.\n\nKnown child rooms, shrines, courtyards, verandas, gates, cremation points, gardens, caves, platforms, photo positions and other micro-sites remain losslessly preserved beneath the parent as M1, M2, M3... They do NOT get separate Mark A/B/C decisions merely because the research database stores them separately.\n\n## Separate decision number only when\nA child is physically a separate destination requiring a materially separate visit, route, access decision, or meaningful travel detour.\n\n## Decision effect\nIf parent = A, all known relevant child micro-sites are automatically included as visit-guide targets subject to access and authenticity. They remain individually documented for the final A-site guide.\n\nIf parent = B/C, child findings remain preserved and may later justify an upgrade, but Mark is not asked to score each child separately.\n\n## Example\nNKB Vrindavan Ashram = one A decision.\nUnder it: Mahasamadhi/cremation site, Maharajji office/room, courtyard, Hanuman sanctum, gate and any other known meaningful micro-sites = M1...Mn.\n\nFouzdar Kunj = one A decision.\nUpper room + semicircular veranda = M1/M2.\n\nVrindavan Railway Station = one A decision.\nNearby unresolved Kriya-initiation micro-location = M1 under station visit rather than a second travel stop.\n\n## Presentation requirement\nBefore presenting a cluster to Mark, collapse the decision surface to parent visit-units FIRST. Then describe all known micro-sites under each parent. Do not make Mark repeatedly score the same physical terrain.\n"}
